              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__ret, &__tmp, 8)`, the function is copying 8 bytes of data from `__tmp` to `__ret`. If `__ret` is not large enough to hold 8 bytes, this could result in a buffer overflow, potentially leading to arbitrary code execution, data corruption, or program crashes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a possible fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(__ret) >= 8) {\n    memcpy(&__ret, &__tmp, 8);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `memcpy` is only called if `__ret` is large enough to hold 8 bytes of data. If it is not, an error is handled instead.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "9afa02c0-c8ae-33a7-ae07-5f1ff09316ab",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__tmp, &__val, 8)`, the function is copying 8 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `&__val` to the location pointed to by `&__tmp`. If `__tmp` is not large enough to hold 8 bytes of data, this could result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(__tmp) >= 8) {\n    memcpy(&__tmp, &__val, 8);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, the size of `__tmp` is checked before calling `memcpy` to ensure that it is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "ab2511d7-d1b1-34c8-b4c4-f51a9df0132a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(__ret, &__tmp, 8)`, the function is copying 8 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `&__tmp` to the location pointed to by `__ret`. If the size of the buffer at `__ret` is less than 8 bytes, this will result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `memcpy_s`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h>\n\n// Ensure __ret is at least 8 bytes\nif (sizeof(__ret) >= 8) {\n    memcpy_s(__ret, sizeof(__ret), &__tmp, 8);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed to use it.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d73d8779-1647-3e41-86f2-908e9b9669b7",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__tmp, __val, 8)`, the function is copying 8 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `__val` to the location pointed to by `__tmp`. If the size of `__tmp` is less than 8 bytes, this could result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(__tmp) >= 8) {\n    memcpy(&__tmp, __val, 8);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, `memcpy` is only called if `__tmp` is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "d824e45a-3420-3a9d-8e9c-6550442b19a5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(__expected, &__old, 8)`, the function is copying 8 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `&__old` to the location pointed to by `__expected`. If the size of the `__expected` buffer is less than 8 bytes, this could result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `memcpy_s`:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(__expected);\nif(size >= 8) {\n    memcpy_s(__expected, size, &__old, 8);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional libraries are needed for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "570a99a9-4bd1-3692-92cb-f56b48efdfe5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__old, __expected, 8)`, there is no check to ensure that the size of `__expected` is not larger than the size of `__old`. If `__expected` is larger than `__old`, this could lead to a buffer overflow, which could potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source data and the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(__old);\nif (size <= 8) {\n    memcpy(&__old, __expected, size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first get the size of `__old` and then check if it is less than or equal to 8. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it is not, we handle the error in an appropriate manner.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "82f77372-c0ba-3cd7-b9d1-35294d7c3161",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__tmp, __desired, 8)`, if the size of `__desired` is more than 8 bytes, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, system crashes, and unauthorized code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source data before copying it to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = sizeof(__desired);\nif (size <= 8) {\n    memcpy(&__tmp, __desired, size);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this fix, we first check the size of `__desired` before copying it to `__tmp`. If the size of `__desired` is more than 8 bytes, we handle the error instead of copying the data.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "13ebd497-3145-32cb-a6af-2213b54813d6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__ret, &__tmp, 4)`, the function is copying 4 bytes of data from the location pointed to by `&__tmp` to the location pointed to by `&__ret`. If the size of `__ret` is less than 4 bytes, this could result in a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the destination buffer before calling `memcpy`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `memcpy`, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s`, which include a length parameter to prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif(sizeof(__ret) >= 4) {\n    memcpy(&__ret, &__tmp, 4);\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this version, the size of `__ret` is checked before calling `memcpy` to ensure that it is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the code example to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be available at a later date. Always refer to the official OWASP and CWE websites for the most up-to-date information."
