            {
              "id": "e9e2701e-73f2-3a2c-8a45-6fd38b700647",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. It should be within the acceptable range and not controlled by user input or external sources.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns NULL, it means that the memory allocation failed. Ignoring this can lead to null pointer dereferencing.\n3. Always free the memory allocated by `malloc()` using `free()` to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Define a maximum buffer size\n#define MAX_BUFF_SIZE 1024\n\nvoid function(size_t buff_size) {\n    if (buff_size > MAX_BUFF_SIZE) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    void* buffer = malloc(buff_size);\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Use buffer...\n\n    free(buffer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the standard C library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "da463fe1-c6fe-3822-977c-c0620c2e2f80",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If the size of the memory to be allocated is controlled by the user or an external input, it can lead to several vulnerabilities such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or Denial of Service (DoS) attacks. \n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate the size of the memory to be allocated. It should be within the acceptable range and not controlled by user input or external sources.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc()`. If it returns NULL, it means that the memory allocation failed. Ignoring this can lead to null pointer dereferencing.\n3. Always free the memory allocated by `malloc()` using `free()` to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```c\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Define a maximum buffer size\n#define MAX_BUFF_SIZE 1024\n\nvoid function(size_t buff_size) {\n    if (buff_size > MAX_BUFF_SIZE) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    void* buffer = malloc(buff_size);\n    if (buffer == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n        return;\n    }\n\n    // Use buffer...\n\n    free(buffer);\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the standard C library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
