              "id": "741f8586-bd29-362c-b7ff-ce53c17da880",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsscanf` function is used, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if not used carefully. This function does not perform bounds checking, and thus can result in writing beyond the end of the allocated space for the buffer.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Instead, use safer alternatives that do perform bounds checking. In the case of `vsscanf`, a safer alternative could be `vsscanf_s` (in Microsoft's C library), which does perform bounds checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vsscanf` function with a safer alternative. For example, if you are using Microsoft's C library, you could use `vsscanf_s` instead:\n\n```cpp\n__res = vsscanf_s(__s, __format, __va);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `vsscanf` function is part of the C standard library (`<cstdio>` in C++, `<stdio.h>` in C), so this library is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "0d2073c7-abfc-3d68-a249-0f56634e3dd9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, `memcpy(&__r, __p, sizeof(__r))`, if the size of `__p` is larger than `__r`, it can lead to a buffer overflow.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, system crashes, and unauthorized code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the source data is not larger than the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the source data before copying it to the destination buffer.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nif (sizeof(__p) <= sizeof(__r)) {\n    memcpy(&__r, __p, sizeof(__r));\n} else {\n    // Handle the error condition\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check if the size of `__p` is less than or equal to the size of `__r`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy` operation. If it is not, we handle the error condition appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the C Standard Library, so no additional library dependencies are required for the provided code snippet.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "fd626de7-17c7-339f-b6b0-9278b778418e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the `vsnprintf` function is used, which is a variadic function that writes formatted output from a variable argument list to a string. If not used properly, it can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer alternatives to these functions, such as the \"bounded\" versions of the same functions, or functions that perform automatic bounds checking. In this case, you can use `vsnprintf_s` or `vsnprintf_l` which are safer versions of `vsnprintf`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `vsnprintf` function with `vsnprintf_s` or `vsnprintf_l`:\n\n```cpp\n__res = vsnprintf_s(__s, __n, _TRUNCATE, __format, __va);\n```\n\nor\n\n```cpp\n__res = vsnprintf_l(__s, __n, __format, locale, __va);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `stdio.h`: This library is required for the `vsnprintf`, `vsnprintf_s`, and `vsnprintf_l` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "1dbf5fbc-d6b5-3251-b8c9-996c405cd073",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions that can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, integer overflows, etc. In this case, the function `mbsrtowcs()` is used, which can lead to buffer overflow if not used correctly.\n\nThe `mbsrtowcs()` function converts a sequence of multibyte characters to a sequence of wide characters. If the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the converted characters, a buffer overflow can occur.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking. Use safer alternatives whenever possible. Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the converted characters plus a null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace `mbsrtowcs()` with a safer alternative such as `mbsrtowcs_s()`, which includes a parameter for the size of the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\n#include <locale.h>\n#include <wchar.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nsize_t len = mbstowcs(NULL, __src, 0) + 1;\nwchar_t* __dest = (wchar_t*)malloc(len*sizeof(wchar_t));\nmbstate_t __ps;\nconst char* temp = __src;\nmbsrtowcs(__dest, &temp, len, &__ps);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- locale.h\n- wchar.h\n- stdlib.h\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
