            {
              "id": "c7c54caa-69a2-3bf1-9b44-c7a22f68d6f6",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc()` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory. If not used properly, it can lead to vulnerabilities such as buffer overflows, memory leaks, and null pointer dereferences. This can lead to crashes, incorrect program behavior, or even allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always check the return value of `malloc()`. If the allocation fails, `malloc()` will return a null pointer. Using this pointer without checking can lead to null pointer dereferences.\n\n2. Always initialize the memory returned by `malloc()`. Uninitialized memory can contain sensitive information from previous allocations.\n\n3. Always free memory allocated with `malloc()` when you're done using it. Failing to do so can lead to memory leaks.\n\n4. Be careful with the size argument to `malloc()`. If the size is user-controlled, it can lead to integer overflows and buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to use `malloc()` safely:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t size) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle allocation failure...\n        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n    }\n    memset(ptr, 0, size);\n    return ptr;\n}\n\nvoid use_memory() {\n    size_t size = 100; // Make sure this is not user-controlled\n    char* buffer = (char*)safe_malloc(size);\n    // Use buffer...\n    free(buffer);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we check the return value of `malloc()`, initialize the memory with `memset()`, and free the memory with `free()` when we're done.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for `malloc()`, `free()`, and `exit()`, and the `string.h` library for `memset()`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
